    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "During the Fourier Transform process, the waveform is decomposed into its constituent frequencies using a weighted integration. The formula is based on the fact that any sinusoidal waveform of a given frequency can be expressed as a sum of sinusoids of fundamental frequency and integer multiples of this frequency, through phasor addition or multiplication by the Euler's formula.\n\nIn more mathematical detail, for a discrete-time signal x[n], the Fourier Transform (in the frequency domain) is given by the Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT):\n\nX[k] = ∑[n=0 to N-1] x[n] * e^(-j * 2 * π * k * n / N)\n\nWhere X[k] represents the discrete-time Fourier Transform, x[n] is the input discrete-time signal, k is the frequency index, n is the time index, and N is the length of the input signal.\n\nThe key insight is that, when we evaluate the DFT, we're effectively computing the sum of products of the signal and sine and cosine functions of different frequencies (determined by k). \n\nFor a pure sinusoidal signal sine(ω0 * t), assume the input x[n] is approximately constant between each sample (Digitization of a continuous",
